# Belt-and-suspenders backstop for the inline `# trivy:ignore:<ID>` comments
# already placed directly in terraform/*.tf. Plain .trivyignore format used
# deliberately, not .trivyignore.yaml -- there's a documented, reproducible
# GitHub issue (aquasecurity/trivy-action#348) where AVD-AWS-0132 specifically
# does not get suppressed via the YAML variant. Using the older, simpler
# format sidesteps that known bug entirely rather than hoping it doesn't
# apply here too.
#
# Each ID's real justification lives as a comment directly above the
# relevant resource/block in the .tf file itself -- this file exists only
# so the suppression definitely takes effect even if inline comment
# placement on nested ingress/egress blocks turns out not to be supported
# the same way top-level resource block placement is.

# EC2 broad egress -- Docker Hub, package updates, Let's Encrypt, No-IP.
# See terraform/security-groups.tf for full reasoning.
AWS-0104

# SSH ingress CIDR -- Trivy can't evaluate the gitignored prod.tfvars value.
# See terraform/security-groups.tf for full reasoning.
AWS-0107

# Public IP on the app subnet -- this instance IS the public entry point,
# no ALB/NAT in front of it. See terraform/networking.tf for full reasoning.
AWS-0164

# S3 SSE-S3 vs SSE-KMS -- cost/complexity tradeoff, not a clear-cut
# necessity like the others. See terraform/s3.tf for full reasoning and the
# upgrade path if you decide this one's worth revisiting.
AWS-0132
